of justice.—{{smaller|'''A court in banc''', a court in which the full bench of judges is present: as, before the ''court in banc''.—'''A sitting in banc''', a session of court held by all the judges or by a quorum of them.—'''Days in banc.''' See ''day''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''bancal'''<sup>1</sup> (bang′kal), ''n.'' [F., prop. adj., bandy-legged.] A saber more curved than usual, as if in imitation of the simitar; specifically, the saber of this form worn by officers of the first French republic and empire, during 1792–1810.

'''bancal'''<sup>2</sup> (bang′kal), ''n.'' [E. Ind.] A weight equal to about 1 pound, used in India.

'''banco''' (bang′kō), ''n.'' [It., a bank, bench, counter, < ML. ''bancus'': see ''bank''<sup>1</sup>.] In ''com.'', the money in which the banks of some countries keep or kept their accounts, in contradistinction to the current money of the place. {{smaller|The distinction was more necessary when the currency consisted, as it often did, of clipped, worn, and foreign coins.—'''Banco mark.''' See ''mark banco'', under ''mark''.}}

'''band'''<sup>1</sup> (band), ''n.'' [< ME. ''band'', ''bande'', also ''bond'', ''bonde'' (> mod. E. ''bond'', the same word, now partly discriminated in use), < AS. *''band'' = OS. ''band'' = OFries. ''band'' = D. ''band'' = OHG. MHG. ''bant'', G. ''band'' = Icel. Sw. ''band'' = Dan. ''baand'', a band, a tie, a neut. noun (in D. and G. also masc.), developing in later use a great variety of particular senses, and merged in ME. with the synonymous ''bend'', ''bende'', ''bænde'', < AS. ''bend'', rarely ''bænd'', in mod. E. prop. ''bend'', and with the slightly different ''bande'', E. ''band''<sup>2</sup>, a strip, hoop, etc., derived through the F. from the same ult. source, namely, Teut. (AS., etc.) ''bindan'' (pret. ''band''), E. ''bind'': see ''bind'', ''bend''<sup>1</sup>, ''bend''<sup>2</sup>, ''bend''<sup>3</sup>, and cf. ''bond''<sup>1</sup>, ''band''<sup>2</sup>, ''band''<sup>3</sup>.] '''1.'''

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|And Pharaoh-nechoh put him in ''bands'' at Riblah.
{{right|2 Ki. xxiii. 33.}}}}
{{smaller block|And suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken: and immediately all the doors were opened, and every one’s ''bands'' were loosed.
{{right|Acts xvi. 26.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Dol.'' In chains of adamant?
''Mam.'' Yes, the strongest ''bands''.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, iv. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' That by which loose things of the same or a similar kind are bound together. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') The tie of straw used in binding sheaves of wheat or other grain. (''b'') In ''bookbinding'', one of the cords, tapes, or strips of parchment which hold together the several sections of the sewed book. The thread is drawn from within each section around or over the bands.}}—'''3.''' That which connects; a connecting piece, or means of connection; that which connects or unites the several parts of a complex thing.
{{smaller block|The body, by joints and ''bands'' . . . knit together, increaseth with the increase of God.
{{right|Col. ii. 19.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He [hope] is a flatterer,
A parasite, a keeper-back of death,
Who gently would dissolve the ''bands'' of life.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., ii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller|Specifically—(''a''†) In ''logic'', the copula. [Rare.]}}
{{smaller block|A simple Axiome is that, the ''band'' whereof is a Verbe.
{{right|''T. Spencer'' (1628), Logick, p. 160. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The metallic sleeve which binds the barrel and stock of a musket together. (''c'') One of two pieces of iron fastened to the bows of a saddle to keep them in place. (''d'') A leaden came. See ''came''<sup>2</sup>. (''e''†) A hyphen.}}—'''4.''' A binding or uniting power or influence: as, a ''band'' of union. [Now usually ''bond''.]
{{smaller block|I drew them with cords of a man, with ''bands'' of love.
{{right|Hos. xi. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Land of my sires! what mortal hand
Can e’er untie the filial ''band''</poem>}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|That knits me to thy rugged strand?
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., vi. 2.}}}}
'''5.''' An obligation imposing reciprocal, legal, or moral duties: as, the nuptial ''bands''. [Now usually ''bond''.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Here’s eight that must take hands,
To join in Hymen’s ''bands''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, v. 4.}}}}
'''6†.''' A binding promise or agreement; a bond or security given.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Adr.'' Tell me, was he arrested on a ''band''?
''Dro. S.'' Not on a ''band'', but on a stronger thing.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iv. 2.}}
<poem>You know my debts are many more than means,
My ''bands'' not taken in, my friends at home
Drawn dry with these expenses.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''), Noble Gentleman, i. 1.}}}}
'''7†.''' A surety; a bondsman.
{{smaller block|<poem>Since faith could get no credit at his hand,
I sent him word to come and sue my ''band''.</poem>
{{right|''Churchyard'', Challenge (ed. 1778), p. 152.}}}}
'''8†.''' A covenant or league. [Scotch.]—{{smaller|'''False bands''', in ''bookbinding'', strips of leather or strands of twisted cord, pasted across the inner side of the backs of books, and afterward molded in high relief to give the appearance of bands of unusual thickness or strength.—'''Raised bands''', in ''bookbinding'', strips of leather or braided cord of unusual thickness, fastened on the outside of the sewed sheets of a book-back, making a noticeable projection on the back, and intended to give increased strength to sewing.}}

'''band'''<sup>2</sup> (band), ''n.'' [< ME. ''bande'', < OF. ''bande'', earlier ''bende'', mod. F. ''bande'' = Pr. ''benda'' = Sp. ''banda'', ''venda'' = Pg. ''banda'' = It. ''banda'' and ''benda'', dial. ''binda'', a band, strip, side, etc., in various particular senses, < OHG. ''binda'', ''binta'', MHG. G. ''binde'', f., a band, fillet, tie, cravat (cf. D. ''bind'', neut., a crossbeam, joint,

