'''3.''' The form of collar commonly worn by men and women in the seventeenth century in western Europe. {{smaller|It was originally starched, and fixed in a half-erect position, nearly like the ruff, which it superseded, and was often of lace and of immense size. Afterward it was turned down over the shoulders, and called a ''falling-band.''}}
{{smaller block|<poem>This ''band''
Shews not my neck enough.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Volpone, iii. 2.}}
Kissing your finger that hath the ruby, or playing with some string of your ''band.'' ''B. Jonson'', Cynthia’s Revels, ii. 1.

The next that mounted the Stage was an Under-Citizen of the Bath, a Person remarkable among the inferior People of that Place for his great Wisdom and his Broad ''Band.''
{{right|''Steele'', in Dobson, p. 452.}}}}

'''4.''' The linen ornament worn about the neck, with the ends hanging down in front, by certain Protestant clergymen. {{smaller|It was prescribed by Queen Elizabeth as a part of the every-day dress of Anglican ecclesiastics. [Now only in the plural.]}}

'''5.''' In ''mining'', a layer of rock interstratified with the coal; sometimes, as in Cumberland, England, the coal itself.—{{smaller|'''Band of rock,''' a phrase sometimes used for ''bed of rock.'' See ''blackband.''—'''Gastroparietal band, hypopharyngeal band, illoparietal band, iliotibial band,''' etc. See the adjectives.}}

'''band'''<sup>3</sup> (band), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''bend'', < late ME. ''bande'', also ''bende'', < OF. and F. ''bande'' = Pr. Sp. It. ''banda'' (ML. ''bandum, bandus''; so G. ''bande'', D. ''bande'', now ''bende'', Dan. ''bande'', Sw. ''band'', after Rom.), a band or company, < OHG. ''bant'', OS. OFries., etc., ''band'', a band or tie, the sense of ‘company’ being developed first in Rom.: see ''band''<sup>1</sup>, ''band''<sup>2</sup>, and cf. the doublet

