'''band'''<sup>3</sup> (band), ''v.'' [< ''band''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To unite in a troop, company, or confederacy: generally reflexive.
{{smaller block|They ''band'' themselves with the prevalent things of this world to overrun the weak things which Christ hath made choice to work by. {{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, ii. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Among the sons of morn, what multitudes
Were ''banded'' to oppose his high decree.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 717.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Band'' them into pueblos; make them work; and, above all, keep peace with the whites.
{{right|''Mrs. H. Jackson'', Ramona, v.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To unite; associate; confederate for some common purpose.
{{smaller block|<poem>With them great Ashur also ''bands'',
And doth confirm the knot.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Ps. lxxxiii. 29.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The great lords
''Banded'', and so brake out in open war.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Coming of Arthur.}}}}
{{smaller block|The weak will ''band'' against her when she becomes too strong. {{right|''R. H. Stoddard'', Guests of State.}}}}

'''band'''<sup>4</sup> (band), ''n.'' [Local E., perhaps a particular use of ''band''<sup>2</sup>, a strip, or possibly of early mod. E. ''bande'', < ME. ''bande'', var. of ''bonde'', a bound, limit: see ''bound''.] A ridge of a hill: commonly applied in the English lake district to a long ridge-like hill of minor height, or to a long narrow sloping offshoot from a higher hill or mountain. ''N. E. D.''

'''band'''<sup>5</sup>†. An obsolete or Scotch preterit of ''bind''.

